Create an empty dictionary.

Initially:

{}


for i, num in enumerate(nums):

enumerate gives both the index and the value.

Example:

i = 0
num = 2

complement = target - num

For the first element:

target = 9
num = 2

complement = 7


We need 7 to complete the pair.

if complement in seen:

Have we already seen 7?

Initially:

No
seen[num] = i

Store the current value and its index.

{
2:0
}


Next iteration:

num = 7

Need = 2

Dictionary:

{
2:0
}

We have already seen 2, so return:

[0,1]

Complexity

Time:

O(n)

Space:

O(n)


Java Solution
----------------
import java.util.HashMap;

class Solution {

    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {

        HashMap<Integer, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {

            int complement = target - nums[i];

            if (seen.containsKey(complement)) {
                return new int[] { seen.get(complement), i };
            }

            seen.put(nums[i], i);
        }

        return new int[] {};
    }
}

Pattern Recognition

Whenever you see phrases like:

Find a pair
Target sum
Complement
Two numbers add up to X

Think:

HashMap + One Pass

This is your first DSA pattern.

Common Mistakes
Returning the numbers instead of the indices.
Adding the current number to the HashMap before checking for its complement.
Using two nested loops when an O(n) solution is expected.
Forgetting that there is usually exactly one valid answer in this problem.

